\begin{abstract}
This white sheet presents the design and mathematical framework for a multi-physics simulator that couples vascular tissue dynamics, biorheological fluid flow, and astrophysical density fields within a unified Hamiltonian architecture. Biological state vectors $(q_{bio}, p_{bio})$, astrophysical flux data (FITS cubes), and interaction terms are combined into a composite Hamiltonian:
\[
H = H_{bio} + H_{astro} + H_{interaction}.
\]
Chaotic regimes emerging from this coupled system are monitored via Lyapunov divergence and used as a non-deterministic entropy source for cryptographic key generation. The resulting engine---centered on the H-WAVE-CIE cryptographic spine and the Blackwell Encryption Layer (BEL)---produces physics-anchored encrypted simulation streams with provenance and integrity verification. This document specifies the architecture, mathematical basis, and proposed validation pathway for laboratory evaluation.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
Conventional cryptography relies on algorithmic randomness and pseudo-random number generators. This work proposes a physics-anchored cryptographic system in which entropy is derived from chaotic divergence in coupled bio-fluid and astrophysical regimes.

The objective of this document is to define a scientifically structured, testable architecture for the H-WAVE-CIE Fluid \& Tissue Encryption Simulator, enabling laboratories to reproduce, analyze, and validate the system’s Hamiltonian dynamics, chaotic behavior, and cryptographic properties.

