\end{itemize}

This module produces biological state vectors:
\[
(q_{bio}, p_{bio}).
\]

\subsection{Module 2: Hamiltonian Coupling \& Cryptographic Engine}
The composite Hamiltonian is defined as:
\[
H = H_{bio}(q_{bio}, p_{bio}) + H_{astro}(q_{astro}, p_{astro}) + H_{interaction}(q_{bio}, q_{astro}).
\]

State vector:
\[
x = (q_{bio}, p_{bio}, q_{astro}, p_{astro}).
\]

Port-Hamiltonian formulation:
\[
\dot{x} = (J - R)\nabla H(x) + Bu,
\]
where $J$ is the interconnection matrix, $R$ is the dissipation matrix, and $u$ represents external forcing.

Chaotic attractor trajectories are sampled to generate cryptographic keys:
\[
K(t) = f(\text{trajectory}(t), \lambda(t)).
\]

\subsection{Module 3: Integrity \& Decryption}
Lyapunov divergence:
\[
\lambda(t) = \frac{1}{t}\ln\frac{\|\delta x(t)\|}{\|\delta x(0)\|}.
\]

A symplectic leapfrog integrator preserves Hamiltonian structure during simulation. Decryption requires reconstruction of the relevant Hamiltonian regime and key evolution $K(t)$.

\section{Blackwell Encryption Layer (BEL)}
The BEL module converts chaotic attractor coordinates and divergence metrics into cryptographic keys. An XOR-based cipher produces hex-encoded encrypted outputs. A provenance spine records Hamiltonian parameters, initial conditions, divergence metrics, and key evolution history.

